  "research": {
    "area-3": {
      "text": "Why scout here\n\nThis location is near CF Shops at Don Mills, a \"lifestyle centre\" with an open-air, walkable layout that integrates residential and retail spaces.\n\nThe shopping centre features various retail stores, restaurants, and services, including McEwan Fine Foods, Anthropologie, and Eataly.\n\nThe presence of existing food establishments and an emphasis on community connection at CF Shops at Don Mills suggests a potential audience for a new café.\n\nAn indoor playground is likely considered a \"recreation use\" and may also fall under the \"amusement establishment\" licence category, which requires a Zoning Review for a Business Licence.\n\nSuch a business requires a unit suitable for assembly occupancy, with adequate exits, washrooms, and fire safety measures, often necessitating a change-of-use permit and a Building Code review.\n\nIt's crucial to confirm that any potential space not only allows for commercial use but specifically permits an indoor playground business, as zoning categories and definitions can vary.\n\nWithin CF Shops at Don Mills, there are establishments like Kitaya Japanese Takeout, Asian Legend, CoCo, Diwan at the Aga Khan Museum, and Sorento Italian Kitchen.\n\nOther cafes and food spots within CF Shops at Don Mills include Danish Pastry House, Lindt, Pablo's Coffee House, Ice'n Cake, and Demetres.\n\nEataly has also recently opened a location within the shopping centre.\n\nAccess and local context\n\nThe area is well-connected, with CF Shops at Don Mills located at Don Mills Road and Lawrence Avenue East, offering on-site parking and proximity to the Don Valley Parkway (DVP).\n\nPublic transit routes also serve the location.\n\nCF Shops at Don Mills is a mixed-use development with residential and retail spaces, aiming to be a community hub.\n\nWhat to check on a visit\n\nThe physical space available needs to be assessed for its suitability as an \"assembly occupancy,\" including the number of exits, washroom facilities, and potential for fire safety compliance.\n\nInquire about the process for a change-of-use permit if the space was previously used for a different purpose.\n\nInvestigate the local requirements for an \"amusement establishment\" licence and whether the proposed indoor playground concept falls under this category.\n\nIf an outdoor patio is considered for the café, understand the City of Toronto's sidewalk café permit requirements, including size limitations, fencing rules, and any associated fees.",
